\begin_layout Subsubsection
The TargetedWorst crossover method
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The TargetedWorst crossover technique extends the Targeted approach by additionally exploiting information about the classification performance of the generated program.
 While Targeted crossover may select an active semantic codon from the chromosome without considering the performance of individual classes,
 TargetedWorst attempts to concentrate the crossover operation on the chromosome region associated with the class exhibiting the poorest classification performance.
 The motivation behind this strategy is to modify preferentially those parts of the generated classification program that are more likely to contribute to the observed classification errors,
 while leaving better-performing components less affected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each selected chromosome 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{c}$
\end_inset

,
 the corresponding classification program is evaluated and the class exhibiting the worst classification performance is identified.
 The genotype-to-phenotype mapping information is subsequently used to determine the active codons associated with the corresponding part of the classification program.
 An active codon is then selected from this region and its semantic type is determined.
 As in the Targeted technique,
 a donor chromosome 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{r}$
\end_inset

 is selected from the elite fraction of the population,
 and an active codon having the same semantic type is identified in the donor chromosome.
 This establishes semantically compatible positions between the two chromosomes and allows genetic material from a high-quality solution to be transferred specifically to a poorly performing region of 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{c}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A bounded block of genetic material is subsequently transferred from the donor chromosome to 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{c}$
\end_inset

.
 As in Targeted crossover,
 the size of the transferred block is determined using the active-codon structure of the participating chromosomes and is restricted by the predefined maximum block size 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\max}$
\end_inset

.
 The resulting chromosome 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{\mathrm{new}}$
\end_inset

 is then mapped to its corresponding classification program and evaluated on the training set.
 If
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f\left(g_{\mathrm{new}}\right)<f\left(g_{c}\right),
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
the candidate chromosome replaces 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{c}$
\end_inset

;
 otherwise,
 the modification is discarded.
 Accepted improvements are immediately retained and are therefore available to subsequent crossover trials.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consequently,
 TargetedWorst introduces an additional level of guidance compared with Targeted crossover.
 Targeted determines the exchanged genetic material according to semantic information obtained during genotype-to-phenotype mapping,
 whereas TargetedWorst combines this information with class-specific classification performance in order to focus the search on chromosome regions associated with poorly performing parts of the generated classifier.
