\begin_layout Standard
Figure [flowchart.png] presents the general flowchart of the proposed method.
 The process starts with the generation of the initial population of integer-valued chromosomes.
 Each chromosome is mapped to a classification program through the BNF grammar,
 and the program is evaluated on the training set,
 which gives the fitness of the chromosome.
 The standard genetic operators of selection,
 one-point crossover and mutation are then applied to the population.
 After them,
 the additional crossover procedure is executed.
 A small number of chromosomes is randomly selected and,
 for each of them,
 a number of crossover trials is performed.
 In every trial,
 the technique in use,
 Standard,
 Targeted or TargetedWorst,
 produces a new candidate chromosome,
 which is mapped to a classification program through the grammar and evaluated.
 If the candidate achieves a lower error than the current chromosome,
 it replaces it,
 otherwise the current chromosome is kept.
 The process continues with the next trial until all the trials of all the selected chromosomes are completed.
 Finally,
 the termination criterion is checked.
 If it is not satisfied,
 the algorithm returns to the mapping and evaluation of the new population,
 otherwise it terminates and returns the best classification program.
\end_layout

