\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Statistical analysis.

\series default
 The comparison of the methods was based on the average classification error of each method on each dataset,
 so that every dataset provides one observation and 
\begin_inset Formula $N=31$
\end_inset

 observations are available for each method.
 All tests were performed at the significance level 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=0.05$
\end_inset

.
 For every table,
 the Friedman test was first applied in order to examine the hypothesis that all the methods of the table perform equally.
 For each dataset the methods are ranked from the lowest to the highest error,
 with ties receiving the average rank,
 and the test compares the mean ranks of the methods.
 The result is also reported with the Iman-Davenport correction,
 which is less conservative,
 and the strength of the differences is expressed by Kendall's coefficient of concordance 
\begin_inset Formula $W=\chi^{2}/(N(k-1))$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is the number of methods.
 The Nemenyi test was then used to compare all the methods through their mean ranks,
 and two methods are considered different when their mean ranks differ by more than the critical difference 
\begin_inset Formula $CD$
\end_inset

.
 In addition,
 specific pairs of methods were compared with the two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test for paired samples,
 applied to the differences of the errors on the individual datasets,
 where zero differences are discarded and tied absolute differences receive average ranks.
 The p-values of the Wilcoxon tests were adjusted with the Holm method separately for each table,
 over the family of comparisons of that table.
 These are the seven comparisons of STANDARD with each of the other methods in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:methods_comparison"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 and all the pairs of methods in Table 
\begin_inset Formula $\ref{tab:crossover_strategies}$
\end_inset

 (three comparisons) and in Table 
\begin_inset Formula $\ref{tab:diff_NK}$
\end_inset

 (six comparisons).
 Finally,
 for Table 
\begin_inset Formula $\ref{tab:diff_NK}$
\end_inset

,
 the one-sided Page trend test was used to examine whether the error decreases as 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{K}$
\end_inset

 increases,
 together with the Spearman correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{K}$
\end_inset

 and the error in each dataset,
 whose sign was summarized with a sign test.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:methods_comparison"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 compares the proposed method (STANDARD) with the original GenClass method (CLASS) and with the other classifiers over the 31 datasets.
 All values are classification errors,
 so lower values indicate better performance.
 STANDARD attained the lowest average error,
 13.42%,
 followed by CLASS with 16.13%,
 while the average errors of the remaining methods ranged from 33.25% (GEN) to 44.04% (NEAT).
 STANDARD achieved the lowest error in 23 of the 31 datasets,
 CLASS in 3,
 and each of the other methods in at most 2.
 Compared with CLASS,
 the average error decreases by 2.71 percentage points,
 a relative reduction of 16.8%.
 Therefore,
 the new crossover procedure improves the original method on average,
 while the improvement over the other methods is considerably larger.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The critical difference of the Nemenyi test is 
\begin_inset Formula $CD=\frac{q_{\alpha}}{\sqrt{2}}\sqrt{\frac{k(k+1)}{6N}}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 is the critical value of the studentized range distribution for 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 methods,
 and it therefore depends on the number of methods compared.
 For each pair of methods,
 the size of the difference was estimated with the Hodges-Lehmann estimator,
 which is the median of the pairwise averages of the paired differences of the errors,
 and its 95% confidence interval was derived from the Wilcoxon signed-rank test using the normal approximation.
 The effect size of each Wilcoxon test was expressed as 
\begin_inset Formula $r=Z/\sqrt{n}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 is the standardized statistic of the test and 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 is the number of datasets with non-zero difference.
 The confidence intervals of the mean rank and of the mean change in error in Figure 
\begin_inset Formula $\ref{fig:effect_of_NK}$
\end_inset

 were computed with the percentile bootstrap,
 using 10,000 resamples of the datasets with replacement.
 
\end_layout

