 presents the statistical analysis of Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:crossover_strategies"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
 The Friedman test indicates significant differences among the three strategies (
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{2}(2)=22.13$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p=1.6\times10^{-5}$
\end_inset

,
 Iman-Davenport 
\begin_inset Formula $F(2,60)=16.65$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p=1.8\times10^{-6}$
\end_inset

),
 with a moderate effect size (
\begin_inset Formula $W=0.36$
\end_inset

).
 STANDARD has the best mean rank (1.32),
 against 2.27 for TARGETED and 2.40 for TARGETEDWORST.
 Since the critical difference is 0.60,
 the bar of STANDARD does not overlap with those of the other two strategies,
 while the bars of TARGETED and TARGETEDWORST overlap.
 The paired tests agree with this picture.
 STANDARD is significantly better than TARGETED (25 wins and 6 losses,
 median difference 1.62 percentage points,
 95% CI 
\begin_inset Formula $[0.58,2.55]$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p=0.0018$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $r=0.60$
\end_inset

) and than TARGETEDWORST (27 wins and 4 losses,
 median difference 1.93 percentage points,
 95% CI 
\begin_inset Formula $[1.08,2.80]$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p=1.6\times10^{-4}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $r=0.73$
\end_inset

).
 On the contrary,
 TARGETED and TARGETEDWORST cannot be distinguished (15 wins,
 3 ties and 13 losses,
 median difference approximately 0.1 percentage points,
 95% CI 
\begin_inset Formula $[-0.48,0.95]$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p=0.64$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $r=0.09$
\end_inset

).
