\begin_layout Section
Conclusions
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Conclusions"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This work introduced an additional crossover procedure into the GenClass method.
 At every generation,
 after the standard genetic operators,
 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{K}$
\end_inset

 randomly selected chromosomes undergo 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 crossover trials,
 and a candidate chromosome replaces the current one only if it achieves a lower classification error on the training set.
 The procedure was examined in three variants (Standard,
 Targeted and TargetedWorst) on 31 classification datasets.
 The Standard variant combines each selected chromosome with randomly chosen chromosomes of the population using one-point crossover.
 It reduced the average classification error of the original method from 16.13% to 13.42%,
 a relative reduction of 16.8%,
 and obtained the lowest error in 23 of the 31 datasets.
 According to the paired Wilcoxon test with Holm correction,
 the improvement was statistically significant,
 and the margin over all the other classifiers of the comparison was considerably larger.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The comparison of the three variants gave a result that differs from what their design might suggest.
 The Targeted and TargetedWorst variants take donor chromosomes only from the best fraction of the population and transfer a bounded block of codons between semantically compatible positions,
 yet they did not improve on the random exchange of Standard.
 Standard was significantly better than both,
 while the two targeted variants could not be distinguished from each other,
 so the additional focus of TargetedWorst on the worst-performing class brought no measurable benefit over Targeted.
 All three variants obtained a lower average error than the original method (13.42%,
 15.08% and 15.42%,
 against 16.13%),
 but statistical significance against the original method was examined only for Standard.
 A possible explanation is related to the difference in the exchanged material.
 In Standard the donors come from the whole population and the genetic material after a random crossover point is exchanged,
 whereas in the targeted variants the donors come from the best 20% of the population and the exchanged block is limited to at most 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\max}$
\end_inset

 codons.
 The unrestricted exchange may therefore introduce more diverse genetic material,
 which relates to the loss of diversity identified as a limitation of the original method.
 This hypothesis was not tested directly in the present work.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The number of chromosomes participating in the crossover affects the performance,
 mainly at its lowest values.
 A very small number (
\begin_inset Formula $N_{K}=2$
\end_inset

) was significantly worse than the other settings,
 whereas the differences among 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{K}=5$
\end_inset

,
 10 and 15 were small and not statistically significant,
 although 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{K}=15$
\end_inset

 obtained the lowest average error and the best mean rank.
 A moderate value of 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{K}$
\end_inset

 therefore seems sufficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Some limitations should be noted.
 The additional procedure requires up to 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{K}k$
\end_inset

 extra fitness evaluations per generation,
 that is 200 with the parameter values of Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Values-for-the"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 while the standard genetic operators generate 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{s}N_{c}=450$
\end_inset

 offspring per generation.
 Moreover,
 the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\max}$
\end_inset

 were kept fixed and their influence was not examined.
 The conclusions are limited to the datasets,
 the parameter values and the classification task used in the experiments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Several directions for future work arise from the present study.
 First,
 the reasons why the targeted variants did not outperform the random one should be investigated,
 for example by measuring the diversity of the population during the evolution,
 by examining the influence of the elite fraction 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 and of the maximum block size 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{\max}$
\end_inset

,
 and by designing guided operators that preserve diversity or combine random and guided exchanges.
 Second,
 the number of chromosomes 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{K}$
\end_inset

 and the number of trials 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 could be adapted automatically during the evolution instead of being fixed,
 and values of 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{K}$
\end_inset

 larger than 15 could be examined,
 since 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{K}=15$
\end_inset

 had the best mean rank without a statistically established advantage.
 Third,
 the computational cost of the procedure should be measured and analyzed together with possible parallel implementations.
 Finally,
 the procedure could be evaluated on larger datasets,
 on other learning tasks such as regression,
 and in other methods based on Grammatical Evolution.
\end_layout

